# Line endings, because a Windows checkout does not hand back what was stored.
#
# `core.autocrlf` is on by default there — GitHub's `windows-latest` runner has
# it on — so every text file arrives with CRLF. Anything this repository
# compares byte for byte against generated or expected text then fails on
# Windows and nowhere else, which is how `the_generated_file_is_current` came
# to report `src/generated.rs is stale` about a file nobody had touched.

# Ours. Normalised to LF in the repository and delivered as LF everywhere.
*.rs text eol=lf
# cbindgen writes this and CI diffs it against the committed copy.
/crates/tt-ffi/include/sterna.h text eol=lf
# The TTL transcripts, which are the expected output of 53 upstream macros.
/crates/tt-ttl/tests/scripts/*.txt text eol=lf

# And the three families whose bytes are the subject rather than the container.
# Never convert these in either direction.
#
# Upstream's, verbatim, and `vendor/ttpfile/sync.sh --check` compares them
# against the pinned checkout — most of them carry Tera Term's own CRLF.
/vendor/** -text
# Terminal input streams: a CR LF in one of these is data the parser sees.
/oracle/cases/**/input -text
# What `GetPrivateProfile*` really answered, recorded from Windows. One of the
# recorded divergences is *about* line endings.
/ini-audit/win32.txt -text
